ed Chows** or **Short Straight** bonuses.\n\nTo do that, I need to preserve sequences.\n\nTherefore, best discard is a **loose honor or stray terminal**.\n\nAmong options, **J2** stands out:  \n- Not useful for me (cannot form pung)  \n- Extremely low danger (last copy likely already out)  \n- Freeing space to draw better tiles\n\nOther options like B1, B2, B4, B5, B6 are valuable for building chows in Bamboo.\n\nT5 and T8 are mid-suited but tied to opponents\u2019 shapes \u2014 slightly safer, but not as clean as dumping an unused honor.\n\nF2 is seat wind \u2014 worth 2 pts if I make a pung, but I only have one. Unlikely. But still, better than J2.\n\nJ3 has four copies \u2014 could become valuable.\n\nHence, **J2 is the weakest tile in hand**.\n\nReason: J2 is a singleton honor with only one copy remaining unseen (and likely already discarded), making it highly unlikely to be useful to any opponent for a winning hand. It contributes nothing to my potential patterns (no second J2 for pair, no easy path to dragon pung/kong), whereas other tiles like B4-B5-B6 form a core sequence for All Chows. Discarding J2 minimizes risk and streamlines my hand toward a coherent, potentially scoring structure.\n\nAnswer: Play J2"}
